## 2. Uniform analytic grading and pre-first-birth input

Let Q_N,r be the orthogonal Hermitian cluster projectors, E_N,r the exact
no-event Riesz projectors, and U_N^H the complete Hermitian canonical
cluster unitary. Use the exact intertwiner

    J_N=(sum_r E_N,r Q_N,r) U_N^H,
    J_N=U_N^H+O(epsilon^3),
    E_N,r-Q_N,r=O(epsilon^3).                               (4)

The order-two difference vanishes: the new imaginary term is grade
diagonal, so its order-two projector contour is a double pole. Both
projector families and J and J^-1 have uniform ordinary norms. Each exact
block propagator has a common bound for all forward physical times,
since it is the restriction, by J, of the contraction V_N.

Two graded refinements are needed and can be proved directly by the
uniform Neumann contour expansion. These are operator estimates, with
constants independent of S:

    ||E_4,r U4 P4|| <= C epsilon^(r+2), r=1,...,4,             (5)
    ||Pi_l J6^-1 j_i J4 Pi_r||
                     <= C epsilon^abs(l-r+1).               (6)

For (5), introduce a scalar multiplying the loss term. At scalar zero the
product E_4,r U4 vanishes identically. Every surviving coefficient contains
at least one loss insertion, of degree two. In its bare grade-r coordinate
it also contains at least r hops from grade zero. All other insertions are
grade diagonal. Thus that coordinate starts at degree r+2. The whole vector
has the same order because E_4,r is a uniformly bounded graph over Pi_r.
This proves an operator bound, not merely a zero-field matrix element.

For (6), j lowers grade by exactly one where nonzero. Each hop changes it
by one, and every degree-two coefficient is grade diagonal. At least
abs(l-r+1) hops are needed between the two bare coordinates. The analytic
series, its inverse series and its common radius give the stated norm
bound. The identity relating h(-epsilon) to h(epsilon) by (-1)^W preserves
the corresponding parity in these coefficients.

The high components of v4(s) therefore have norms O(epsilon^(r+2)),
uniformly 0<=s<=T, by exact commuting projection and contraction. Its exact
low coordinate a4,e(s)=Pi0 J4^-1 v4(s) obeys

    a4,e'=(-i K D4+B4,e) a4,e,                              (7)

where B4,e is uniformly bounded, including after conjugation by any fixed
power of w=1+sum_edges E_edge^2. This follows from the same contour/polar
series: unit edge shifts have bounded w-power ratios, while W, D/C and
Gamma commute with w. The compensation cancellation isolates KD4 exactly;
all remaining low coefficients are bounded products plus an epsilon^2
uniform remainder.

For completeness, this also supplies a quantitative strong limit:

    sup_(s<=T) ||a4,e(s)-u4(s)||=O(epsilon^2),
    sup_(s<=T) ||w^m a4,e(s)||+||w^m u4(s)|| <= C_(m,T)       (8)

for each fixed nonnegative integer m. Extend spin operators by zero, and
keep the same free KD4 outside the spin box. The difference of every
normalized spin shift from its rotor shift on u is bounded by
C epsilon^2 ||w u||, including the boundary and zero extension. This follows
from 1-sqrt(1-x)<=x and 1_(outside box)<=C epsilon^2 w. Products have the
same estimate. The anticommutator with D/C is also O(epsilon^2 w) on the
limiting orbit. The uniformly bounded epsilon^2 remainder causes no worse
error. Variation of constants against the limiting orbit, with a common
semigroup bound, yields the first estimate in (8). The initial coordinate
differs from Omega by O(epsilon^3). Conjugated interaction-picture Dyson
series yield the second estimate. No global norm convergence of D is used.

The same weighted argument applies to exact N=6 low propagation on every
fixed physical horizon, with arbitrary uniformly weighted initial vectors.

## 3. Uniform birth-source orders, including the second-high cancellation

For all 0<=s<=T, decompose the source L_i v4(s) in the exact N=6 coordinates:

    z_i,r(s)=Pi_r J6^-1 L_i v4(s).

Then the required estimates are

    z_i,0(s)=sqrt(kappa) B_i,S a4,e(s)+O(epsilon^2),
    z_i,1(s)=sqrt(kappa) epsilon R_i u4(s)+O(epsilon^3),
    z_i,2(s)=O(epsilon^4).                                 (9)

The leading low term is uniformly bounded in every fixed w power.
Remainders in (9) are ordinary-norm remainders; no unproved weighted bound
on a long-propagated high component is assumed.

Here is the full grading argument. On a Hermitian canonical low column,

    Pi0 J6^-1 j_i U4 = epsilon B_i,S+O(epsilon^3),
    Pi1 J6^-1 j_i U4 = epsilon^2 R_i,S+O(epsilon^4).          (10)

The minimal second-high coefficient of degree three is

    j_i F^3/6-F j_i F^2/2+F^2 j_i F/2.                     (11)

Applied to P4, it vanishes as an operator. Outward F_c commute with j_i
for c!=a, including overlapping hard-core zero actions. They commute with
one another; F_a^2=0 and j_i P4=0. Thus (11) is the degree-three outward
commutator, with all remote terms cancelling and the local third iterated
commutator vanishing. This argument does not exchange opposite shifts on
one edge, so it holds for the actual finite-spin weights. Grade parity
makes the next possible term degree five. Compensation and loss cannot
enter degree three at grade two: three hops are already necessary.
Consequently Pi2 J6^-1 j_i U4=O(epsilon^5).

Changing U4 to J4 Pi0 changes its bare grade-k component by
O(epsilon^(k+2)) for k>=1 and its grade-zero component by O(epsilon^4),
by the proof of (5). After j and output rotation the grade-l correction
is O(epsilon^(l+3)); after multiplication by epsilon^-1 it is
O(epsilon^(l+2)). Thus it cannot spoil (9) or (10).

For the pre-existing high part E_4,r v4(s), (5)-(6) give a contribution
to z_i,l of order

    epsilon^(r+1+abs(l-r+1)).                              (12)

For l=1 its minimum over r>=1 is three; for l=2 it is four; for l=0 it
is two. These bounds are uniform in s because the exact high components
contract. Finally (8) and the weighted spin-to-rotor bound replace
R_i,S a4,e(s) by R_i u4(s) with O(epsilon^2) error. This proves (9).

In particular ||L_i v4(s)|| is uniformly bounded. The bounded normalization
here is for the raw jump source. No postselection denominator is inserted.

