## 5. First-high integral: recent and older births

The exact propagated first-high coordinate, with its scalar phase removed,
is exp[(t-s)A_e,1/epsilon^2] z_i,1(s). At age epsilon, (9), (13), (15) give

    ||E6,1 x_i(s,s+epsilon)|| <= C epsilon^(9/4).            (17)

For all older ages up to T this same bound is carried by the exact
commuting projection and contraction. For Hermitian Q6,1 the difference
in (4) costs only O(epsilon^3)||x_i||=O(epsilon^3). Therefore

    integral_(0<=s<=t-epsilon) ||Q6,1 x_i(s,t)||^2 ds
                                            =O(epsilon^(9/2)). (18)

Both the ordinary first-high energy and its epsilon^4-scaled second
moment from these older ages vanish, since multiplying (18) by epsilon^-4
gives O(epsilon^(1/2)). This disposes of ages as large as fixed t without
asserting a uniform finite-spin decay gap.

For the recent interval t-epsilon<=s<=t, put tau=(t-s)/epsilon^2, so
0<=tau<=epsilon^-1. The first-high profile, divided by sqrt(kappa) epsilon
and expressed in Hermitian coordinates, differs from
exp(tau Zfast) R_i u4(t) by an error bounded by

    C [epsilon^2 (1+tau)^(3/4)+epsilon^2 tau+epsilon^2].    (19)

The first term is (15); the second uses the bounded strong derivative
of u4(s) to freeze s at t; the third includes the source error in (9),
uniform intertwiners and the Hermitian/no-event projector difference.
The square of (19) integrates over [0,epsilon^-1] to
O(epsilon^(3/2)+epsilon+epsilon^3), which tends to zero. The limiting
profile has a uniform integrable squared norm by (13), so its cross
term with the error also vanishes by Cauchy-Schwarz. The tail above
epsilon^-1 tends to zero. Restoring ds=epsilon^2 d tau gives

    epsilon^-4 sum_i integral_0^t
                ||Q6,1 x_i(s,t)||^2 ds -> kappa I(t).        (20)

On this Hermitian block H6=delta epsilon^-4[I+O(epsilon^2)] in operator
norm. Equation (20) therefore yields

    sum_i integral <Q1 x_i,H6 Q1 x_i> ds -> kappa delta I(t),
    epsilon^4 sum_i integral ||H6 Q1 x_i||^2 ds
                                             ->kappa delta^2 I(t). (21)

These are exact Hermitian block moments; no nonorthogonal Riesz cross
term is discarded in the energy calculation.

## 6. Other bands and the common low energy

The exact second-high coordinates start O(epsilon^4) by (9), and their
semigroups have a common bound. Equation (4) and ||x_i||=O(1) give
||Q6,2 x_i||=O(epsilon^3), uniformly over all s<=t<=T. Thus their total
mean is O(epsilon^2), and their second moment is O(epsilon^-2), negligible
after the epsilon^4 scaling. No numerical gap estimate is used.

For the low coordinate, propagate separately the leading term
sqrt(kappa) B_i,S a4,e(s) and the O(epsilon^2) ordinary remainder in (9).
Call them y_i(s,t) and r_i(s,t). The first is uniformly weighted and
converges strongly, uniformly on the time triangle, to
sqrt(kappa) exp[(t-s)A6] B_i u4(s); the second stays O(epsilon^2).
The weighted Dyson argument in section 2 gives the first assertion;
the exact common semigroup bound gives the second. In Hermitian low
coordinates the extra discrepancy is at most O(epsilon^3)||x_i|| and
can be included in r_i.

The physical low Hamiltonian is

    H6,low=K D6+delta H4,6,S+O(epsilon^2),
    ||H6,low||=O(epsilon^-2),
    ||H6,low y_i||=O(1).                                  (22)

The cross error in its expectation is O(||r_i|| ||H6,low y_i||)=O(epsilon^2),
and the remainder expectation is bounded by
||H6,low|| ||r_i||^2=O(epsilon^2). Its energy-vector norm is O(1),
so the entire low second moment remains bounded. Strong convergence
and common D-weighted bounds imply convergence of the leading electric
expectation by

    |<y,Dy>-<z,Dz>| <= ||y-z|| (||Dy||+||Dz||).

The bounded other terms converge strongly. Dominated integration on the
finite time triangle now gives exactly the common N=6 contribution to
E_rot(t), with no high-energy term hidden in this step.

The same estimates in N=4 show its mean tends to <u4(t),h4 u4(t)>.
Its first-high norm is O(epsilon^3), and aggregate higher-high norm is
O(epsilon^4), uniformly on bounded times by (5) and (4). Its second
moment is O(epsilon^-2), so its epsilon^4-scaled contribution vanishes.
This coarse bound does not require the stronger later N=4 variance
coefficient or the first-high initial-transient decay theorem.

The N=8 energy is exactly zero. Adding the Hermitian band contributions
proves (2) and the second-moment version of (3). The complete mean is
bounded by (2), hence its square times epsilon^4 vanishes. Equation (3)
follows for the full mixed-state variance.

## 7. Limits, checks still owed, and why the route matters

The argument is for a fixed cube, fixed positive parameters and fixed
t>0. Constants can be chosen on compact positive time intervals, but
no statement uniform down to t=0 or out to infinite physical time is
needed here. The initial limiting low mean has no anomaly at t=0;
the t>0 formula concerns a singular continuously injected birth layer.
Do not commute t->0, kappa->0, or S->infinity without a separate proof.

The new load-bearing steps are the source grading (5)-(12), the smooth
input extension (13), and the integrated error estimate (19). Each must
receive selective independent reconstruction before publication. The
prior finite-spin small examples do not prove these uniform estimates.
The earlier five-state cascade is a mechanism control and already has
the limits (2)-(3) in its own parameters, but it is not the full cube.

The full ensemble remains mixed. A positive or divergent variance is
not a lower bound on its quantum Fisher information. The no-first-birth
resource result, if used separately, has a different scale and different
explicit covariance/readout premises. No upper bound on the full actual
Fisher information is asserted here.

This is energy accounting inside the supplied microscopic process. It
does not supply a physical bath/battery whose conserved total generates
that process, select compensation or canonical preparation, identify a
laboratory energy, enlarge the graph, or complete a theory of everything.
It identifies a candidate observable defect of low-state convergence:
an integrable fast tail can leave finite ordinary energy and an exactly
specified divergent variance when fresh high-energy outputs are
continuously injected.

## 8. A trace-class common matter/field description of the energy defect

The same estimates give more than two scalar moments. Map the microscopic
Hermitian first-high block back to the fixed rotor bare-grade space by
the corresponding column of U6^H. Let rho6,high^coord denote that positive
block, extended by zero outside the spin box. Then

    epsilon^-4 rho6,high^coord(t) -> Sigma(t) in trace norm,
    Sigma(t)=kappa sum_i integral_0^infinity
        |exp(tau Zfast) R_i u4(t)><exp(tau Zfast) R_i u4(t)| d tau. (23)

This assertion is an additional candidate consequence, not an independently
checked result. Its proof uses exactly the L^2-in-age estimates above:
for vector paths f and g, the trace norm of their integrated rank-one
difference is bounded by ||f-g||_L2 (||f||_L2+||g||_L2). The old-age trace
is O(epsilon^(1/2)) after scaling by epsilon^-4, and the recent profile
error tends to zero in L^2 by (19). No oscillatory low/high cross-density
limit is asserted. The scalar first-high carrier phase cancels in each
rank-one density before integration.

Sigma is positive trace class, Tr Sigma=kappa I(t), and retains the full
first-high matter/field fiber. Thus every bounded observable on that
fiber has a well-defined leading rare-population coefficient. This is a
supplement to the common low process, not a new normalized replacement
state and not a field-only description.

Since Zfast is bounded, differentiating its semigroup inside the
convergent trace-class integral gives the exact frozen-source identity

    Zfast Sigma+Sigma Zfast^*
                       =-kappa sum_i |R_i u4(t)><R_i u4(t)|. (24)

The upper endpoint vanishes by (13). Taking the trace and using
Gamma6,1=2 Pi_bright and sum_i R_i^*R_i=72I yields

    Tr[Gamma6,1 Sigma(t)]=72 exp(-48 kappa t),
    Tr[Pi_bright Sigma(t)]=36 exp(-48 kappa t).              (25)

This explains the independent PRE's universal lower coefficient: the
total high population contains this exact bright part and a nonnegative
dark part. The latter, and hence Tr Sigma, can depend on the full fast
Hamiltonian, evolved field and original instrument. No equality between
the two instruments is inferred merely from (25).

Equations (24)-(25) are a balance in the frozen fast-age problem. They do
not justify interchanging the epsilon limit with a physical-time
derivative, nor do they themselves account for an external conserved
apparatus energy. The ordinary-energy excess in (2) is delta Tr Sigma,
and the variance coefficient in (3) is delta^2 Tr Sigma. This gives an
explicit candidate common matter/field object governing both observables.
